## What I learned

- A reusable `request()` helper that checks `response.ok` keeps error handling in one place, because `fetch` only rejects on network failures, not on 404 or 500.
- A good UI shows every state (loading, success, error, empty), disables buttons during requests, and uses `textContent` so user text can never inject HTML.
- REST design means plural nouns, the right verbs and status codes (201 for create, 204 for delete, 400/401/403/404 for client errors).
- Many-to-many relationships need a join table, and an index on `(user_id, created_at)` serves the most common query.
- Scaling is about removing single points of failure and using caches, replicas and queues for a read-heavy workload, each with a trade-off.
